<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Detailed Explanations</h2>

<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>Local Coordinate Expression</h3>

<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A Riemannian metric <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> is symmetric and positive-definite; i.e., at <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in{\mathcal{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in{\mathcal{M}}</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="A1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle g_{p}(v,w)=g_{p}(w,v),\quad g_{p}(v,v)\geq 0\ \text{for all }v\in{\mathcal{T}}_{p}{\mathcal{M}},\quad g_{p}(v,v)=0\Leftrightarrow v=0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>for all </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">⇔</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle g_{p}(v,w)=g_{p}(w,v),\quad g_{p}(v,v)\geq 0\ \text{for all }v\in{\mathcal{T}}_{p}{\mathcal{M}},\quad g_{p}(v,v)=0\Leftrightarrow v=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">By identifying <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> with an inner product, we write</p>
<table id="A1.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle\langle v,w\rangle_{g}\coloneq g_{p}(v,w)\quad\text{for any }v,w\in T_{p}{\mathcal{M}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mtext>for any </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle\langle v,w\rangle_{g}\coloneq g_{p}(v,w)\quad\text{for any }v,w\in T_{p}{\mathcal{M}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x^{1},\dots,x^{D})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>D</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x^{1},\dots,x^{D})</annotation></semantics></math> be smooth local coordinates in a neighborhood of <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in{\mathcal{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in{\mathcal{M}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, the coordinate basis for <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{p}{\mathcal{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{p}{\mathcal{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigl(\frac{\partial}{\partial x^{1}}|_{p},\dots,\frac{\partial}{\partial x^{D}}|_{p}\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mrow><mfrac><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mfrac><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>D</mi></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigl(\frac{\partial}{\partial x^{1}}|_{p},\dots,\frac{\partial}{\partial x^{D}}|_{p}\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial}{\partial x^{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial}{\partial x^{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th coordinate vector field.
Tangent vectors <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v,w\in T_{p}{{\mathcal{M}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v,w\in T_{p}{{\mathcal{M}}}</annotation></semantics></math> can be expressed as <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v=\sum_{i=1}^{D}v^{i}\,\frac{\partial}{\partial x^{i}}|_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>D</mi></msubsup><msub><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v=\sum_{i=1}^{D}v^{i}\,\frac{\partial}{\partial x^{i}}|_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w=\sum_{i=1}^{D}w^{i}\,\frac{\partial}{\partial x^{i}}|_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>D</mi></msubsup><msub><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w=\sum_{i=1}^{D}w^{i}\,\frac{\partial}{\partial x^{i}}|_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.
The matrix notation <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> consists of <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math>-elements</p>
<table id="A1.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle g_{ij}(p)=g_{p}\left(\frac{\partial}{\partial x^{i}}|_{p},\frac{\partial}{\partial x^{j}}|_{p}\right)=\left\langle\frac{\partial}{\partial x^{i}}|_{p},\frac{\partial}{\partial x^{j}}|_{p}\right\rangle_{g}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle g_{ij}(p)=g_{p}\left(\frac{\partial}{\partial x^{i}}|_{p},\frac{\partial}{\partial x^{j}}|_{p}\right)=\left\langle\frac{\partial}{\partial x^{i}}|_{p},\frac{\partial}{\partial x^{j}}|_{p}\right\rangle_{g}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">for <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i,j=1,2,...,D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i,j=1,2,...,D</annotation></semantics></math>.
The Euclidean metric is represented by the identity matrix <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>.
The inner product of <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to the Riemannian metric <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> is</p>
<table id="A1.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle g_{p}(v,w)=\sum_{i=1}^{D}\sum_{j=1}^{D}g_{ij}(p)v^{i}w^{j}=v^{T}G_{p}w." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>D</mi></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>D</mi></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>v</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle g_{p}(v,w)=\sum_{i=1}^{D}\sum_{j=1}^{D}g_{ij}(p)v^{i}w^{j}=v^{T}G_{p}w.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>Explanation of Proposition <a href="#Thmproposition1" title="Proposition 1. ‣ Interpretation. ‣ 4 Method ‣ Be Tangential to Manifold: Discovering Riemannian Metric for Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a></h3>

<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When the score function <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> is exact, it is the gradient <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{x_{t}}\log p_{t}(x_{t};\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{x_{t}}\log p_{t}(x_{t};\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> of the log-density <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log p_{t}(x_{t};\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log p_{t}(x_{t};\theta)</annotation></semantics></math>, and its Jacobian <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{x_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{x_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math> equals the Hessian, <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{x_{t}}=\nabla_{x_{t}}\nabla_{x_{t}}\log p_{t}(x_{t};\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{x_{t}}=\nabla_{x_{t}}\nabla_{x_{t}}\log p_{t}(x_{t};\theta)</annotation></semantics></math>, which is symmetric.
In this idealized case, its eigenvectors form an orthonormal basis of the noise space <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We divide these eigenvectors into a basis for the tangent space <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{T}}_{x}{\mathcal{M}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{T}}_{x}{\mathcal{M}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{v_{i}\}_{i=1}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{v_{i}\}_{i=1}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> (with small eigenvalues <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>), and a basis for the normal space <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{N}}_{x}{\mathcal{M}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{N}}_{x}{\mathcal{M}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{v_{j}\}_{j=d+1}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mi>D</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{v_{j}\}_{j=d+1}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math> (with large eigenvalues <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>).
These spaces are orthogonal complements of each other, and the tangent space <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{T}}_{x}\mathbb{R}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{T}}_{x}\mathbb{R}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math> to the noise space <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> can be decomposed into their direct sum, <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{T}}_{x}\mathbb{R}^{D}={\mathcal{T}}_{x}{\mathcal{M}}_{t}\oplus{\mathcal{N}}_{x}{\mathcal{M}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{T}}_{x}\mathbb{R}^{D}={\mathcal{T}}_{x}{\mathcal{M}}_{t}\oplus{\mathcal{N}}_{x}{\mathcal{M}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Any tangent vector <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in{\mathcal{T}}_{x}\mathbb{R}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in{\mathcal{T}}_{x}\mathbb{R}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math> is uniquely decomposed as <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v=v_{\mathcal{T}}+v_{\mathcal{N}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v=v_{\mathcal{T}}+v_{\mathcal{N}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{\mathcal{T}}\in{\mathcal{T}}_{x}{\mathcal{M}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\mathcal{T}}\in{\mathcal{T}}_{x}{\mathcal{M}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{\mathcal{N}}\in{\mathcal{N}}_{x}{\mathcal{M}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\mathcal{N}}\in{\mathcal{N}}_{x}{\mathcal{M}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The squared Jacobian-vector product <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|J_{x_{t}}v\|_{2}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|J_{x_{t}}v\|_{2}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> can be expanded as:</p>
<table id="A1.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle\|J_{x_{t}}v\|_{2}^{2}=\|J_{x_{t}}(v_{\mathcal{T}}+v_{\mathcal{N}})\|_{2}^{2}=\|J_{x_{t}}v_{\mathcal{T}}\|_{2}^{2}+\|J_{x_{t}}v_{\mathcal{N}}\|_{2}^{2}+2\langle J_{x_{t}}v_{\mathcal{T}},J_{x_{t}}v_{\mathcal{N}}\rangle." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle\|J_{x_{t}}v\|_{2}^{2}=\|J_{x_{t}}(v_{\mathcal{T}}+v_{\mathcal{N}})\|_{2}^{2}=\|J_{x_{t}}v_{\mathcal{T}}\|_{2}^{2}+\|J_{x_{t}}v_{\mathcal{N}}\|_{2}^{2}+2\langle J_{x_{t}}v_{\mathcal{T}},J_{x_{t}}v_{\mathcal{N}}\rangle.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Due to the orthogonality of the eigenspaces, the cross term <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle J_{x_{t}}v_{\mathcal{T}},J_{x_{t}}v_{\mathcal{N}}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle J_{x_{t}}v_{\mathcal{T}},J_{x_{t}}v_{\mathcal{N}}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes, and we have</p>
<table id="A1.E15" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="A1.E15X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E15X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\textstyle\|J_{x_{t}}v_{\mathcal{T}}\|_{2}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\textstyle\|J_{x_{t}}v_{\mathcal{T}}\|_{2}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E15X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\textstyle\sum_{i=1}^{d}\lambda_{i}^{2}\langle v,v_{i}\rangle^{2}\approx 0," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></msubsup><mrow><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\textstyle\sum_{i=1}^{d}\lambda_{i}^{2}\langle v,v_{i}\rangle^{2}\approx 0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A1.E15Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E15Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\textstyle\|J_{x_{t}}v_{\mathcal{N}}\|_{2}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\textstyle\|J_{x_{t}}v_{\mathcal{N}}\|_{2}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E15Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\textstyle\sum_{j=d+1}^{D}\lambda_{j}^{2}\langle v,v_{j}\rangle^{2}\gg 0\quad(\text{if }v_{\mathcal{N}}\neq 0)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mo>∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>D</mi></msup><mi>λ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>≫</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>if </mtext><mi>v</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\textstyle\sum_{j=d+1}^{D}\lambda_{j}^{2}\langle v,v_{j}\rangle^{2}\gg 0\quad(\text{if }v_{\mathcal{N}}\neq 0).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Hence, minimizing the squared Jacobian–vector product <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|J_{x_{t}}v\|_{2}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|J_{x_{t}}v\|_{2}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> (under a fixed Euclidean norm of <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>) is dominated by minimizing the normal-space component <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|J_{x_{t}}v_{\mathcal{N}}\|_{2}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|J_{x_{t}}v_{\mathcal{N}}\|_{2}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and essentially encourages the vector <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> to lie in the tangent space <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{T}}_{x}{\mathcal{M}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{T}}_{x}{\mathcal{M}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In practice, diffusion models learn the score function <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> directly, its Jacobian <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{x_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{x_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math> need not be symmetric, and the right singular vectors need not be exactly orthogonal to each other.
Even then, minimizing <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|J_{x}v\|_{2}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|J_{x}v\|_{2}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> still suppresses the component in the subspace spanned by the large right singular vectors and amplifies the component spanned by the small right singular vectors; Proposition <a href="#Thmproposition1" title="Proposition 1. ‣ Interpretation. ‣ 4 Method ‣ Be Tangential to Manifold: Discovering Riemannian Metric for Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> continues to hold in this generalized sense.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Experimental Setup</h2>

<section id="A2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.1 </span>DDIM Inversion</h3>

<div id="A2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Naive encoding of an original image is to add Gaussian noise as in the forward process <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q({x_{t}}\mid{x}_{t-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q({x_{t}}\mid{x}_{t-1})</annotation></semantics></math>, which is stochastic and often yields poor reconstructions.
To accurately invert the reverse process and recover the specific noise map associated with a given image, <em id="A2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">DDIM Inversion</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mokady et al., 2023</a>)</cite> is widely used.
The deterministic version (<math id="A2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta=0</annotation></semantics></math>) of DDIM can be regarded as an ordinary differential equation (ODE) solved by the Euler method <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib79" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2021a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib80" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2021b</a>)</cite>.
In the limit of infinitesimally small timesteps, the ODE is invertible.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Concretely, setting <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}=0</annotation></semantics></math> in Eq. (<a href="#S3.E6" title="In Denoising Diffusion Implicit Models and Inversion. ‣ 3.2 Diffusion Models ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ Be Tangential to Manifold: Discovering Riemannian Metric for Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) gives</p>
<table id="A2.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t-1}=a_{t}x_{t}+b_{t}\epsilon_{\theta}(x_{t},t)=x_{t}+(a_{t}-1)x_{t}+b_{t}\epsilon_{\theta}(x_{t},t)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t-1}=a_{t}x_{t}+b_{t}\epsilon_{\theta}(x_{t},t)=x_{t}+(a_{t}-1)x_{t}+b_{t}\epsilon_{\theta}(x_{t},t),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{t}=\sqrt{\alpha_{t-1}/\alpha_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{t}=\sqrt{\alpha_{t-1}/\alpha_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{t}=-\sqrt{\alpha_{t-1}(1-\alpha_{t})/\alpha_{t}}+\sqrt{\vphantom{/}1-\alpha_{t-1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{t}=-\sqrt{\alpha_{t-1}(1-\alpha_{t})/\alpha_{t}}+\sqrt{\vphantom{/}1-\alpha_{t-1}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This can be viewed as an ODE with the time derivative <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a_{t}-1)x_{t}+b_{t}\epsilon_{\theta}(x_{t},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a_{t}-1)x_{t}+b_{t}\epsilon_{\theta}(x_{t},t)</annotation></semantics></math> solved by the Euler method with the unit step size.
With a sufficiently small timestep size,</p>
<table id="A2.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}=\frac{x_{t-1}-b_{t}\epsilon_{\theta}(x_{t},t)}{a_{t}}\approx\frac{x_{t-1}-b_{t}\epsilon_{\theta}(x_{t-1},t)}{a_{t}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>≈</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}=\frac{x_{t-1}-b_{t}\epsilon_{\theta}(x_{t},t)}{a_{t}}\approx\frac{x_{t-1}-b_{t}\epsilon_{\theta}(x_{t-1},t)}{a_{t}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">since <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\theta}(x_{t},t)\approx\epsilon_{\theta}(x_{t-1},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\theta}(x_{t},t)\approx\epsilon_{\theta}(x_{t-1},t)</annotation></semantics></math>.
The deterministic forward process iteratively applies the update rule in Eq. (<a href="#A2.E17" title="In B.1 DDIM Inversion ‣ Appendix B Experimental Setup ‣ Be Tangential to Manifold: Discovering Riemannian Metric for Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>) to a sample <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=0</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> and obtains the noisy image <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math>, from which the deterministic reverse process reconstructs the original <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> up to numerical errors.
This inversion procedure substantially improves the fidelity of reconstructions and subsequent interpolations.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.2 </span>Comparison Methods</h3>

<section id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Linear Interpolation.</h4>

<div id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Once samples are noised via DDIM Inversion, one can perform straightforward linear interpolation (LERP) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ho et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, by treating the noise space at fixed time <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=\tau&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=\tau&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> as a linear latent space.
Given samples <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}^{\scriptscriptstyle(0)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo maxsize="0.710em" minsize="0.710em">(</mo><mn mathsize="0.710em">0</mn><mo maxsize="0.710em" minsize="0.710em">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}^{\scriptscriptstyle(0)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}^{\scriptscriptstyle(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo maxsize="0.710em" minsize="0.710em">(</mo><mn mathsize="0.710em">1</mn><mo maxsize="0.710em" minsize="0.710em">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}^{\scriptscriptstyle(1)}</annotation></semantics></math> in the data space, the deterministic forward process obtains their noised versions <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\tau}^{\scriptscriptstyle(0)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="0.710em" minsize="0.710em">(</mo><mn mathsize="0.710em">0</mn><mo maxsize="0.710em" minsize="0.710em">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\tau}^{\scriptscriptstyle(0)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\tau}^{\scriptscriptstyle(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="0.710em" minsize="0.710em">(</mo><mn mathsize="0.710em">1</mn><mo maxsize="0.710em" minsize="0.710em">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\tau}^{\scriptscriptstyle(1)}</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.
A linear interpolation in that space is given by</p>
<table id="A2.E18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{x}_{\tau}^{\scriptscriptstyle(u)}=(1-u)x_{\tau}^{\scriptscriptstyle(0)}+ux_{\tau}^{\scriptscriptstyle(1)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="0.710em" minsize="0.710em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.710em">u</mi><mo maxsize="0.710em" minsize="0.710em">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="0.710em" minsize="0.710em">(</mo><mn mathsize="0.710em">0</mn><mo maxsize="0.710em" minsize="0.710em">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="0.710em" minsize="0.710em">(</mo><mn mathsize="0.710em">1</mn><mo maxsize="0.710em" minsize="0.710em">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{x}_{\tau}^{\scriptscriptstyle(u)}=(1-u)x_{\tau}^{\scriptscriptstyle(0)}+ux_{\tau}^{\scriptscriptstyle(1)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> is the interpolation parameter.
Then, one applies the deterministic reverse process from <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=\tau</annotation></semantics></math> back to <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=0</annotation></semantics></math> to obtain a sequence of interpolated images <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}^{\scriptscriptstyle(u)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo maxsize="0.710em" minsize="0.710em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.710em">u</mi><mo maxsize="0.710em" minsize="0.710em">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}^{\scriptscriptstyle(u)}</annotation></semantics></math> in the data space.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Spherical Linear Interpolation.</h4>

<div id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An alternative is spherical linear interpolation (SLERP) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib79" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2021a</a>)</cite>, which finds the shortest path on a sphere in the noise space:</p>
<table id="A2.E19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\tau}^{\scriptscriptstyle(u)}=\frac{\sin((1-u)\theta)}{\sin{(\theta)}}x_{\tau}^{\scriptscriptstyle(0)}+\frac{\sin(u\theta)}{\sin{(\theta)}}x_{\tau}^{\scriptscriptstyle(1)}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="0.710em" minsize="0.710em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.710em">u</mi><mo maxsize="0.710em" minsize="0.710em">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="0.710em" minsize="0.710em">(</mo><mn mathsize="0.710em">0</mn><mo maxsize="0.710em" minsize="0.710em">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="0.710em" minsize="0.710em">(</mo><mn mathsize="0.710em">1</mn><mo maxsize="0.710em" minsize="0.710em">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\tau}^{\scriptscriptstyle(u)}=\frac{\sin((1-u)\theta)}{\sin{(\theta)}}x_{\tau}^{\scriptscriptstyle(0)}+\frac{\sin(u\theta)}{\sin{(\theta)}}x_{\tau}^{\scriptscriptstyle(1)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta=\arccos\left({\frac{(x_{\tau}^{\scriptscriptstyle(0)})^{\top}x_{\tau}^{\scriptscriptstyle(1)}}{\|x_{\tau}^{\scriptscriptstyle(0)}\|\|x_{\tau}^{\scriptscriptstyle(1)}\|}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>arccos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta=\arccos\left({\frac{(x_{\tau}^{\scriptscriptstyle(0)})^{\top}x_{\tau}^{\scriptscriptstyle(1)}}{\|x_{\tau}^{\scriptscriptstyle(0)}\|\|x_{\tau}^{\scriptscriptstyle(1)}\|}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>.
This procedure preserves the norms of the noisy samples <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\tau}^{\scriptscriptstyle(u)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="0.710em" minsize="0.710em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.710em">u</mi><mo maxsize="0.710em" minsize="0.710em">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\tau}^{\scriptscriptstyle(u)}</annotation></semantics></math>, yielding more natural interpolations than LERP.
Note that SLERP assumes that <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\tau}^{\scriptscriptstyle(0)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="0.710em" minsize="0.710em">(</mo><mn mathsize="0.710em">0</mn><mo maxsize="0.710em" minsize="0.710em">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\tau}^{\scriptscriptstyle(0)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\tau}^{\scriptscriptstyle(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="0.710em" minsize="0.710em">(</mo><mn mathsize="0.710em">1</mn><mo maxsize="0.710em" minsize="0.710em">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\tau}^{\scriptscriptstyle(1)}</annotation></semantics></math> are drawn from a normal distribution, which holds only for a sufficiently large <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> (typically, <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=T</annotation></semantics></math>).
Nonetheless, SLERP is often applied at moderate <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.3 </span>Prompt Adjustment</h3>

<div id="A2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To improve the quality of interpolations, we adopt the prompt adjustment proposed by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib95" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al. (2025)</a></cite>.
Internally in Stable Diffusion v2.1-base <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rombach et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, a text prompt <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> is first encoded into a text embedding <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> using CLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">Radford et al., 2021</a>)</cite>.
To better align the text embedding <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> with a given pair of images <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}^{\scriptscriptstyle(0)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo maxsize="0.710em" minsize="0.710em">(</mo><mn mathsize="0.710em">0</mn><mo maxsize="0.710em" minsize="0.710em">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}^{\scriptscriptstyle(0)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}^{\scriptscriptstyle(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo maxsize="0.710em" minsize="0.710em">(</mo><mn mathsize="0.710em">1</mn><mo maxsize="0.710em" minsize="0.710em">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}^{\scriptscriptstyle(1)}</annotation></semantics></math>, we adjust the text embedding <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> in a similar way to textual inversion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gal et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.
Namely, the text embedding <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> is updated to minimize the DDPM loss in Eq. (<a href="#S3.E5" title="In Reverse Process. ‣ 3.2 Diffusion Models ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ Be Tangential to Manifold: Discovering Riemannian Metric for Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) for 500 iterations for image interpolation and 1,000 iterations for video frame interpolation.
We use AdamW optimizer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Loshchilov &amp; Hutter, 2019</a>)</cite> with a learning rate of 0.005.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Also following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib95" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al. (2025)</a></cite>, we do not use CFG (i.e., set <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w=0</annotation></semantics></math> in Eq. (<a href="#S3.E8" title="In Conditioning and Guidance ‣ 3.2 Diffusion Models ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ Be Tangential to Manifold: Discovering Riemannian Metric for Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>)) but use the following negative prompt <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{\text{neg}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mtext>neg</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{\text{neg}}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{\text{neg}}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mtext>neg</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{\text{neg}}=1</annotation></semantics></math>: “A doubling image, unrealistic, artifacts, distortions, unnatural blending, ghosting effects, overlapping edges, harsh transitions, motion blur, poor resolution, low detail.”</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Details of Experiments</h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section provides additional details of the experiments in Section <a href="#S5" title="5 Experiments ‣ Be Tangential to Manifold: Discovering Riemannian Metric for Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.
All experiments were conducted on a single NVIDIA A100 GPU.</p>
</div>
<section id="A3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.1 </span>Details of Synthetic 2D Dataset</h3>

<section id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Dataset.</h4>

<div id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We construct a two-dimensional C-shaped distribution as follows.
We start with an axis-aligned ellipse with semi-axes <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1.0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.0</annotation></semantics></math> (along <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>) and <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1.2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.2</annotation></semantics></math> (along <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>).
To open the “C”, we remove all points in a <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm 30^{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><msup><mn>30</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm 30^{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math> wedge centered on the positive <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-axis.
We then add isotropic Gaussian perturbations with standard deviation <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.001</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.001</annotation></semantics></math> per coordinate to each point.
From the resulting distribution, we draw 100,000 samples.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Network.</h4>

<div id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The noise predictor <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> is composed of three linear layers of hidden width 512 with SiLU activation functions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Elfwing et al., 2017</a>)</cite>.
The network takes a tuple of a data point <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and a normalized time <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> as input.
We set the number of steps to <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=1,000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1,000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=1,000</annotation></semantics></math>.
We trained this network for 1,000 epochs using the AdamW optimizer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Loshchilov &amp; Hutter, 2019</a>)</cite> with a batch size of 512.
The learning rate follows cosine annealing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Loshchilov &amp; Hutter, 2017</a>)</cite>, decaying from <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-3}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> without restarts.
For stability, we apply gradient-norm clipping with a threshold of <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1.0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Implementation Details.</h4>

<div id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Fig. <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Be Tangential to Manifold: Discovering Riemannian Metric for Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> (left), we visualize the interpolation between <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}^{\scriptscriptstyle(0)}=(0.0,1.15)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo maxsize="0.710em" minsize="0.710em">(</mo><mn mathsize="0.710em">0</mn><mo maxsize="0.710em" minsize="0.710em">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1.15</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}^{\scriptscriptstyle(0)}=(0.0,1.15)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}^{\scriptscriptstyle(1)}=(-0.8,-0.6)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo maxsize="0.710em" minsize="0.710em">(</mo><mn mathsize="0.710em">1</mn><mo maxsize="0.710em" minsize="0.710em">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.8</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.6</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}^{\scriptscriptstyle(1)}=(-0.8,-0.6)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=100</annotation></semantics></math> discretization points.
Comparison methods include Linear Interpolation (LERP) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ho et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, Spherical Linear Interpolation (SLERP) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib79" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2021a</a>)</cite>, and density-based interpolation based on the metric proposed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib95" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al. (2025)</a></cite>.
We used the DDIM Scheduler <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib79" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2021a</a>)</cite> and operated in the noise space at <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=0.02T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>0.02</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=0.02T</annotation></semantics></math>.
For our method and the density-based interpolation, we find the geodesic paths by minimizing the energy functional <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E[\gamma]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E[\gamma]</annotation></semantics></math>.
Both paths are initialized using SLERP and updated using Adam optimizer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kingma &amp; Ba, 2015</a>)</cite> for 1,000 iterations with a learning rate of <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.2 </span>Datasets for Image Interpolation</h3>

<div id="A3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The animation subset of MorphBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib96" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2023</a>)</cite> is a dataset of pairs of images obtained via image editing.
Each pair is associated with a text prompt; we used the provided prompts as the condition <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Animal Faces-HQ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Choi et al., 2020</a>)</cite> is a dataset of high-resolution images of animal faces.
From this dataset, we randomly selected 50 pairs of dog images and 50 pairs of cat images with LPIPS below 0.6 to ensure semantic similarity.
We used the text prompts “a photo of a dog” for dog images and “a photo of a cat” for cat images.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">CelebA-HQ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karras et al., 2018a</a>)</cite> is a high-resolution dataset of celebrity faces.
We randomly sampled 50 male pairs and 50 female pairs, again with LPIPS less than 0.6, and condition on “a photo of a man” and “a photo of a woman,” respectively.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Additional Results</h2>

<section id="A4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.1 </span>Additional Qualitative Results for Image and Video Frame Interpolation</h3>

<div id="A4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we provide additional qualitative results.
Figures <a href="#A4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ D.1 Additional Qualitative Results for Image and Video Frame Interpolation ‣ Appendix D Additional Results ‣ Be Tangential to Manifold: Discovering Riemannian Metric for Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> and <a href="#A4.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ D.1 Additional Qualitative Results for Image and Video Frame Interpolation ‣ Appendix D Additional Results ‣ Be Tangential to Manifold: Discovering Riemannian Metric for Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> provide more examples of image interpolation and video frame interpolation, which complement Fig. <a href="#S5.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ Metrics. ‣ 5.2 Image Interpolation ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Be Tangential to Manifold: Discovering Riemannian Metric for Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and Fig. <a href="#S5.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Experimental setup. ‣ 5.3 Video Frame Interpolation ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Be Tangential to Manifold: Discovering Riemannian Metric for Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> in the main text, respectively.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A4.F5" class="ltx_figure">
<table id="A4.F5.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="A4.F5.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
